# Day 41

**42km (on bike) + 30km (on foot) / 12 hours**

This was a long day since the cycling of 42 km or so only took 4 hours so may as well push on to the town ahead but that was a lengthy stretch. 

The day began with collecting the bikes to begin again where we left off yesterday and it initially felt like I was riding again for the first time; all the comfort from yesterday was gone and my confidence was shattered, especially so after some windy sections where I struggled to navigate effectively. Additionally the track had quite a lot of uphill sections which were tough even when it was only a gentle ascent.

![A nice stream being passed over. Wouldn't be as nice if I had to ride over it](./stream.avif)

After a while of this the track finally turned to being mostly downhill, it was quite impressive following the old tramway used for transporting logs out from the old forestry operations. There was a bunch of info on the history of the place which I was often travelling too quickly to read and fully appreciate. There were also some impressive suspension bridges today as well.

![The old tramway dug out the hill to make the line flatter which is convenient if you're cycling it](./tramway.avif)

![A cool suspension bridge across a valley](./suspension-bridge.avif)

Later on there was also a spiral round and the trail went through a dark tunnel which was mighty impressive but I wasn't brave enough to just ride through in the dark.

![It was darker than this photo implies but you could see the outline of the walls so most likely would be okay](./tunnel.avif)

After a final stretch next to a farm which seemed to go on for a while we came out next to a campsite. However it was only around noon, much too early to finish so after a decent lunch it was time to leave the bikes behind, pack the bags up and continue on foot once again. There's not a huge amount to say about this walk; it was a long slog on the road and not particularly exciting. It was also quite hot and I didn't bring sufficient water so I had to ration it. When we did eventually get into the town there was a drinking fountain and after filling up I probably drunk a whole litre right off the bat.

Unfortunately the accommodation was a few kilometres out of town so the sun was going down when we finally got in to pitch the tents for the night. A long day but not too bad all things considered. A huge amount of distance covered too which is quite nice.
